ただけで息が切れた。しかし、それは体力不足や学校の後の疲れだと思われていた。", "アメリアさんの足に原因のわからないあざが出て、ジェマさんは何かおかしいと気づいた。10日以内に白血病とわかり、彼女は治療のために入院した。今は回復して、3か月ごとに検査を受けながら学校に戻っている。", "小児腫瘍科のボブ・フィリップス教授は、続いたり大きくなったりする症状の組み合わせに注意するよう言う。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出急性髓性白血病。据母亲杰玛说，起初她容易疲倦，稍微运动就喘不过气，但大家以为只是体力不足或放学后的疲劳。", "阿米莉亚腿上出现了不明原因的淤青，杰玛才意识到不对劲。10天内确诊为白血病，她住院接受治疗。如今她已缓解，每三个月检查一次，也回到了学校。", "儿科肿瘤专家鲍勃·菲利普斯教授提醒，要留意持续存在或不断变大的症状组合。如果孩子极度疲倦、脸色苍白、出现无法解释的淤青、越长越大的肿块，最好去看医生。"], "body": "13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは去年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母親のジェマさんによると、最初は疲れやすく、少し運動しただけで息が切れた。しかし、それは体力不足や学校の後の疲れだと思われていた。\n\nアメリアさんの足に原因のわからないあざが出て、ジェマさんは何かおかしいと気づいた。10日以内に白血病とわかり、彼女は治療のために入院した。今は回復して、3か月ごとに検査を受けながら学校に戻っている。\n\n小児腫瘍科のボブ・フィリップス教授は、続いたり大きくなったりする症状の組み合わせに注意するよう言う。", "body_zh": "13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出急性髓性白血病。据母亲杰玛说，起初她容易疲倦，稍微运动就喘不过气，但大家以为只是体力不足或放学后的疲劳。\n\n阿米莉亚腿上出现了不明原因的淤青，杰玛才意识到不对劲。10天内确诊为白血病，她住院接受治疗。如今她已缓解，每三个月检查一次，也回到了学校。\n\n儿科肿瘤专家鲍勃·菲利普斯教授提醒，要留意持续存在或不断变大的症状组合。如果孩子极度疲倦、脸色苍白、出现无法解释的淤青、越长越大的肿块，最好去看医生。", "text": "13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは去年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母親のジェマさんによると、最初は疲れやすく、少し運動しただけで息が切れた。しかし、それは体力不足や学校の後の疲れだと思われていた。 アメリアさんの足に原因のわからないあざが出て、ジェマさんは何かおかしいと気づいた。10日以内に白血病とわかり、彼女は治療のために入院した。今は回復して、3か月ごとに検査を受けながら学校に戻っている。 小児腫瘍科のボブ・フィリップス教授は、続いたり大きくなったりする症状の組み合わせに注意するよう言う。", "vocab": [{"word": "診断", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诊断", "note": "常用搭配：〜される（被诊断）", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "症状", "pos": "noun", "zh": "症状", "note": "医疗话题高频词", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "白血病", "pos": "noun", "zh": "白血病", "note": "病名，注意汉字写法", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "疲れ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疲劳", "note": "动词「疲れる」的名词形", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "あざ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "淤青", "note": "常写作平假名", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "入院", "pos": "noun", "zh": "住院", "note": "反义词：退院", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "回復", "pos": "noun", "zh": "恢复、缓解", "note": "〜する（恢复）", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "教授", "pos": "noun", "zh": "教授", "note": "用于称呼大学或医院专家", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "注意", "pos": "noun", "zh": "注意、留意", "note": "〜する（注意、提醒）", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "生存率", "pos": "noun", "zh": "存活率", "note": "医疗新闻常见词", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "専門家", "pos": "noun", "zh": "专家", "note": "泛指某一领域的专业人士", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "亡くなる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "去世", "note": "比「死ぬ」更委婉的说法", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for", "summary_original": "Although rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK.", "body_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for\nBy\nCamilla Horrox\nHealth reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nAlthough rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK - yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.\nAbout 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed with cancer each year, according to Cancer Research UK.\nThirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll, who is now in remission, was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) last year.\nHer mum Gemma recalls the initial symptoms being easy to overlook.\nThere was the tiredness, she says, and being out of breath after mild exercise but both were put down to being unfit and just post-school fatigue.\nIt was only when Amelia started presenting with bruises Gemma knew something wasn't right - within 10 days Amelia got her leukaemia diagnosis.\nWhat are the symptoms to look out for?\nProf Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, says it's key for parents to look out for clusters of symptoms that may be persisting, getting worse or bigger.\nConsider contacting a doctor over the following:\nExtreme tiredness\nLooking really pale\nBruises that you can't explain\nLumps and bumps that are getting bigger or not going away and growing over time\nCancer in children develops much more quickly than in adults - effectively due to them growing.\nExperts like Phillips at CCLG, the Children and Young People's Cancer Association, are urging people to follow a national campaign aiming to raise awareness and improve diagnosis times.\nIt includes a rule-of-three for professionals: If a child has had three persistent symptoms, or has visited their doctor three times, or has had three weeks of unexplained symptoms consider making a referral.\nAlthough, it's important to note survival rates have risen significantly since the 1970s, with eight out of 10 children surviving beyond five to 10 years or more after diagnosis.\nFor Amelia, her cancer journey began with a single red bruise that came out of nowhere, says Gemma.\nThree days later, she had bruises down both of her shins on both legs, which were sore and raised.\n\"We'd gone to the opticians for a routine appointment and they noticed that she'd got some burst blood vessels but they weren't sure why,\" she adds.\nWithin 10 days, doctors had confirmed what type of leukaemia Amelia had and she was admitted to hospital to begin treatment.\n\"On the day that they confirmed what she had, I sat her down and she said to me 'have I got cancer?' and I said yes you have. She asked me if she was going to die.\"\n\"I said no, they're doing everything they can, they've got a plan in place and the treatment is going to do everything it can to beat the cancer.\"\nAmelia is currently in remission, undergoing regular bone marrow and blood tests every three months, and has returned to school.\nPhillips says survival rates for childhood cancer in the UK are over 80% but that even quicker diagnosis would lead to less intensive treatments.\n'Raising awareness is his legacy'\nAround the UK, about half the children diagnosed with cancer get their diagnosis within two weeks, he says.\n\"It's pretty quick but we want the other half to not have the wait of 16, 18, 20, 25 weeks from those first symptoms to get a diagnosis.\"\nCancer Research UK data shows that there are more than 400,000 new cases of cancer diagnosed every year in the UK, with only 54% being told within the 28-day NHS target window, according to the British Medical Journal (BMJ).\nAn additional 2,300 teenagers and young adults (aged 15–24) are diagnosed each year.\nLynsey Swinscoe is mum to Jake, who died of soft tissue cancer aged 13. Jake was diagnosed with stage three, fusion positive alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma in July 2023.\nBefore his diagnosis, Jake's initial symptoms of a stuffy nose and watery eyes were put down to hayfever, and falling asleep after school was thought to be just end-of-term tiredness.\n\"It was only when he started to get a swelling across the bridge of his nose that we thought right, we need to take him to the GP,\" Lynsey says.\n\"But at that point we didn't really think the fatigue and hayfever symptoms were related.\"\nA mass was found in Jake's nose and nasal cavity and he was diagnosed with soft tissue sarcoma. He completed nine rounds of chemotherapy and proton beam radiation.\nAfter suffering seizures and feeling very unwell from the chemotherapy, Jake opted out of continuing treatment.\nHe passed away peacefully at home on 26 April 2024, just two weeks before his 14th birthday.\n\"I think the main thing with Jake is he was not scared to die. We had lots of conversations about dying and what it might feel like to die and the only thing that he was sort of scared about was, 'are all the people going to remember me? Is everyone just going to forget who I am?'\n\"That's why I'm doing this, to make sure that people know who Jake is, to make sure people know how incredible he was. And so with that comes the raising awareness, which I think, that's his legacy.\"\nRelated topics\nLeukaemia\nCancer\nChild health\nNHS\nHealth\nChildhood cancer\nMore on this story\nMother writes book to help children with cancer\nPublished\n4 October\nGirl, 7, rings bell to mark end of cancer treatment\nPublished\n1 October\n'This doesn't happen to us' - mum on child's cancer\nPublished\n12 September", "fetched_at": "2